.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

The return of the marine layer (around 1600 feet per 12Z OAK
Upper Air Sounding) resulted in widespread coverage of stratus
this morning, with the exception of the East Bay interior valleys.
Since then, clouds are quickly retreating to the coast and are
forecast to linger off of the Central Coast for much of the day.
However, a weak frontal boundary approaching the North Bay is
likely to bring a return of clear skies off of the North Bay coast
this afternoon.

Despite mostly sunny sky conditions across the interior, today
will be much cooler than previous days as the region remains in
between mid/upper level cut-off low off of the British Columbia
coast and high pressure confined to southern California, Nevada,
and Arizona. Less confidence tonight in the amount of stratus that
returns to the region as the aforementioned frontal boundary
moves southward and then weakens.

The cut-off low will shift southward before moving into the
Pacific Northwest Saturday night and into Sunday. As such,
westerly to northwesterly winds will increase tomorrow afternoon
and persist through Saturday night ahead of yet another frontal
boundary. The strongest wind gusts will be between 25 to 35 mph
with favored gaps, passes, and coastal regions gusts up to 45 mph.
This cold front will sweep across the North Bay and Bay Area
Saturday night into Sunday with no measurable precipitation
forecast.

