.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

In wake of the frontal passage, offshore flow is forecast from
Sunday night and into Monday night as 925 mb winds become
northerly to northeasterly. However, the offshore flow is not
forecast to be much greater than 20 kt (23 mph) at 925 mb,
limiting fire weather concerns. Onshore winds are forecast to
persist in the lower elevations with the strongest in the early
afternoon and through the evening hours. Thus, the marine layer
looks to hold around 1500 feet in depth through the weekend. As
mentioned in the Fire Weather section of the AFD below, areas
above the marine layer often experience nocturnal drying (during
the overnight hours). That will be the case during this offshore
event. The trough axis is forecast to move into the northern
Rockies and the northern Plains early next week, diminishing the
offshore wind potential.

Lingering troughiness along the West Coast will maintain cooler
than average temperatures for much of next week. This will give
way to temperatures warming into the 60s to lower 70s along the
northwest facing coastal locations and low to upper 70s around the
Santa Cruz region, bayshore, and coastal adjacent valleys.
Meanwhile, interior valleys are forecast to reach into the upper
70s to mid 80s with the warmest interior valleys of the Central
Coast reaching into the low 90s.


