.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1030 AM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026

Stratus coverage is decreasing across the Bay Area but persists
directly along the coast and along the Monterey Peninsula.
Cloud cover is expected to continue decreasing through the
remainder of the morning with low to moderate confidence in the
timing of stratus return tonight. HREF keeps stratus more confined
to the coast and along the SF Bay shoreline with the marine layer
~1000 ft. The marine layer did overperform this morning (12Z OAK
sounding reported it to a depth of 1500 ft) which resulted in more
widespread cloud cover. If the marine layer again overperforms
tonight then we may see more expansive cloud cover than currently
forecast. Generally expecting IFR to LIFR CIGs with lower CIGs
expected along the coast. Gusty onshore winds return this
afternoon with winds decreasing overnight. There is an early
signal for gusts between 25 to 30 knots on Saturday so have
started including after 18Z Saturday for 30 hour TAF sites.

Vicinity of SFO...VFR persists through tonight with a low to
moderate chance for stratus to impact the airport overnight.
Confidence is low that stratus will reach SFO as the latest
guidance keeps stratus more confined to the coast and mainly
impacting OAK in the SF Bay. If stratus does reach SFO it will
likely be early Saturday morning and clear by noon at the latest.
Strong onshore winds are expected Saturday afternoon/evening with
gusts to around 30 knots, potentially up to 35 knots, likely.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Stratus has cleared at SNS but is
persisting over MRY. Guidance shows some potential for stratus to
persist at MRY through the day but there may be a brief period of
clearing/VFR conditions this afternoon. IFR-LIFR stratus returns
overnight and persists through the end of the TAF period. Breezy
onshore winds return during the day before decreasing overnight.

